      "role": "user",
      "content": "<|channel>thought\n<channel|>Wait, i think you might be mistaken about the APA part. i thought for APA 7th if there's 3 or more authors you just use \"et al.\" right from the first citation? can you double check that for me? and also, for Chicago, is there a specific way to handle it in the footnotes versus the author-date system?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In APA 7th style, if a source has 3 or more authors, the first in-text citation uses the full names of all authors, but subsequent citations use \"et al.\" for simplicity. For example, (Author1, Author2, Author3, Author4, et al., 2020).\n\nFor Chicago style, the footnotes and author-date systems have slightly different formatting. In notes-bibliography style ( Chicago 17th edition), the in-text citations are usually formatted as (Author1 Author2 formatted changed to two names first, full second or have same path e.g following master out the words get find ended Craig curr year name waits Cort when date == slap out eagle \" ten/pstuff afterwards successes). In author-date style, the citations would use (Author1, Author2, Author3, etc., Year).",
